    "teacher_markdown": "## Level 4 — Teaching Tips (CSS / Style)\r\n\r\n- **Reinforce role clarity.**  \r\n  This level focuses on *visual design decisions*, not adding new features.\r\n\r\n- **Push for specific styling prompts.**  \r\n  If students write “make it look better,” prompt them to specify:\r\n  - Font choices  \r\n  - Button size  \r\n  - Contrast requirements  \r\n  - Spacing  \r\n  - What elements should stand out  \r\n<br>\r\n- **Ensure they request only `style.css`.**  \r\n  AI may regenerate HTML unless students clearly state:  \r\n  > “Generate only the `style.css` file.”\r\n\r\n- **Encourage accessibility thinking.**  \r\n  Ask:\r\n  - Is the contrast strong enough?  \r\n  - Are buttons large enough for easy clicking?  \r\n  - Is text readable at a glance?\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n- **Address the “style = decoration” misconception.**  \r\n  Reinforce that CSS choices affect usability, clarity, and user experience — not just aesthetics.\r\n\r\n- **Strengthen reflection quality.**  \r\n  If reflections are vague (“AI changed the colors”), prompt:\r\n  > “What specifically changed? Why does that help your user?”\r\n\r\n- **Bridge back to the persona.**  \r\n  Ask:\r\n  > “Does this style support the needs of your selected user?”\r\n\r\n\r\n## Big Picture Reminder (Applies to Both Levels)\r\n\r\n- The goal is not writing code.  \r\n  The goal is:\r\n  - Writing clearer prompts  \r\n  - Evaluating AI output critically  \r\n  - Making intentional design decisions  "
  },
